<p style="font-size: 21px">Teaching a teenager to drive is one of the few parenting jobs where a mistake can change a life in a couple of seconds. And the help you get for it is surprisingly thin.</p>
<p>Your state tells you when your kid can get a permit, how many hours to log, whether they need driver’s ed, and when they can take the road test. All of that answers a legal question: when is this person eligible to drive alone?</p>
<p>You get the harder question.</p>
<p style="font-size: 24px; font-weight: 800; color: #0A6CA5; line-height: 1.3; letter-spacing: -0.01em">Is my kid actually ready to drive without me?</p>
<p>Those aren’t the same thing. I’ve done this four times. The first time, I didn’t know the difference.</p>
<p>Here’s the good news. The permit period gives you something nobody else gets: months of real driving with an experienced adult sitting right there. This guide is about using that time well.</p>

<section class="q" id="why-teens-are-at-risk">
<h2>Why are teen drivers at greater risk?</h2>
<p class="answer">Mostly, they haven’t seen enough yet.</p>
<p>The mechanics come fast. Steering, braking, parking, signaling: you can watch those improve week to week.</p>
<p>Judgment is slower. As an experienced driver, you’re tracking a dozen things without noticing: the pedestrian drifting toward the crosswalk, brake lights four cars up, the car nosing out of a driveway, the truck leaning toward your lane. Nobody taught you that list. You picked it up over years.</p>
<div style="display: flex; gap: 20px; align-items: center; background: #E6F5FC; border-radius: 14px; padding: 18px 22px; margin: 6px 0 22px">
<div style="font-size: 46px; font-weight: 800; color: #0A6CA5; letter-spacing: -0.03em; line-height: 1; white-space: nowrap">4×+</div>
<div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; color: #2E3B45">Per mile driven, drivers 16 to 19 crash at more than four times the rate of drivers 20 and older. <span style="color: #6B7A85">— Insurance Institute for Highway Safety</span></div>
</div>
<p>That isn’t because teenagers are bad drivers. It’s because the skills that make someone a good driver come from exposure, and they haven’t had much.</p>
<p>So supervised practice isn’t really about teaching them to work the car. It’s about giving them enough miles to start understanding what’s happening around it.</p>
</section>

<section class="q" id="when-risk-is-highest">
<h2>When is a new driver most at risk?</h2>
<p class="answer">Passing the road test doesn’t end the dangerous period. It starts it.</p>
<p>NHTSA notes that teens rarely crash while an adult is supervising. Their highest crash rates come in the first six months of driving alone.</p>
<p>Think about what changes that day. Yesterday, you were in the passenger seat watching traffic, spotting trouble and quietly fixing things. Today, they’re doing all of it by themselves.</p>
<p>Their experience didn’t jump when the license printed. Their responsibility did.</p>
<p class="pull">Treat the license as a handoff, not a graduation.</p>
</section>

<section class="q" id="how-many-hours">
<h2>How many hours should a teenager practice?</h2>
<p class="answer">Start with your state’s number, then plan past it.</p>
<p>The Insurance Institute for Highway Safety’s best-practice standard is at least 70 supervised hours, and most states require less. All four of my kids learned in Ohio, which asks for 50. I live in Iowa now, which asks for 20. Same kind of kid, very different number.</p>
<div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 12px; margin: 4px 0 24px">
<div style="border: 1px solid #DCE7EE; border-radius: 12px; padding: 14px 16px"><div class="eyebrow">Iowa</div><div style="font-size: 30px; font-weight: 800; color: #0A6CA5; line-height: 1.1">20 hrs</div><div style="height: 8px; border-radius: 4px; background: #EDF3F7; margin-top: 10px"><div style="width: 28%; height: 8px; border-radius: 4px; background: #41B6E5"></div></div></div>
<div style="border: 1px solid #DCE7EE; border-radius: 12px; padding: 14px 16px"><div class="eyebrow">Ohio</div><div style="font-size: 30px; font-weight: 800; color: #0A6CA5; line-height: 1.1">50 hrs</div><div style="height: 8px; border-radius: 4px; background: #EDF3F7; margin-top: 10px"><div style="width: 71%; height: 8px; border-radius: 4px; background: #41B6E5"></div></div></div>
<div style="border: 1px solid #0A6CA5; border-radius: 12px; padding: 14px 16px; background: #F6FAFC"><div class="eyebrow" style="color: #0A6CA5">IIHS best practice</div><div style="font-size: 30px; font-weight: 800; color: #0A6CA5; line-height: 1.1">70 hrs</div><div style="height: 8px; border-radius: 4px; background: #EDF3F7; margin-top: 10px"><div style="width: 100%; height: 8px; border-radius: 4px; background: #0A6CA5"></div></div></div>
</div>
<p>And hours are a blunt tool anyway. Picture two kids with 50 hours each. One drove the same three routes all year. The other drove neighborhoods, busy commercial roads, rural two-lanes, interstates, rush hour, dark and rain.</p>
<p>Same number in the log. Very different drivers.</p>
<p>That’s why we talk about <strong>coverage, not just hours</strong>. Repetition builds skill. Variety builds judgment, because driving almost never hands you the same situation twice. <a href="/guide/">Look up your state’s requirements →</a></p>
</section>

<section class="q" id="structured-practice">
<h2>Does structured practice actually make a difference?</h2>
<p class="answer">Yes, and there’s good evidence for it.</p>
<p>Researchers at Children’s Hospital of Philadelphia tested a program called the TeenDrivingPlan, built to get families practicing in more kinds of places, including at night and in bad weather, with parents more involved as coaches. When the teens were later evaluated on the road, the difference showed up:</p>
<div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 12px; margin: 4px 0 22px">
<div style="background: #F6FAFC; border: 1px solid #E7EEF3; border-radius: 12px; padding: 16px 18px"><div style="font-size: 40px; font-weight: 800; color: #45535E; line-height: 1">15%</div><div style="font-size: 15px; color: #45535E; margin-top: 6px">had the road assessment stopped for unsafe driving <b style="color: #212529">without</b> the plan</div></div>
<div style="background: #E6F5FC; border: 1px solid #CDEBF8; border-radius: 12px; padding: 16px 18px"><div style="font-size: 40px; font-weight: 800; color: #0A6CA5; line-height: 1">6%</div><div style="font-size: 15px; color: #2E3B45; margin-top: 6px">had it stopped <b style="color: #212529">with</b> the plan</div></div>
</div>
<p>That doesn’t mean there’s one perfect program. It means practicing on purpose is a different thing from piling up miles. Know what you’re working on. Go find different kinds of roads. Coach a little less each month.</p>
</section>

<section class="q" id="what-to-experience">
<h2>What should teens experience before they drive alone?</h2>
<p class="answer">More than the parking lot and the drive to school.</p>
<p>Most families start in the right spot: a parking lot or a quiet street, where a new driver can learn how the car responds without too much coming at them. The trouble is staying there. Comfort can look a lot like competence when the scenery never changes.</p>
<p>Before driving alone, your teen should have real time in each of these:</p>
<div class="envs">
<div class="env"><b>Commercial roads</b><span>Cars pulling in and out of driveways, extra lanes, signals to plan for, pedestrians, unprotected left turns.</span></div>
<div class="env"><b>Highways</b><span>Merging at speed instead of hesitating, judging a moving gap, trucks, staying sharp for a long stretch, picking the right exit.</span></div>
<div class="env"><b>Rural roads</b><span>Higher speeds, hills and curves that hide what’s ahead, narrow shoulders or none, deer, tractors, gravel.</span></div>
<div class="env"><b>Night</b><span>The CDC puts teens’ fatal crash rate at night at about three times that of adults 30 to 59, per mile driven.</span></div>
<div class="env" style="grid-column: 1 / -1"><b>Bad weather</b><span>Rain, glare, fog, snow.</span></div>
</div>
<p>Early on, protecting a new driver from the hard stuff makes sense. Later, avoiding it creates its own risk: they meet it for the first time alone.</p>
<p class="pull">The first time your teen merges into fast traffic, drives a dark country road or gets caught in a downpour shouldn’t be the first time you’re not in the car.</p>
<p>Add it gradually. Not all at once. But add it.</p>
</section>

<section class="q" id="skills-that-matter">
<h2>What skills matter most?</h2>
<p class="answer">The ones they use without you saying anything.</p>
<p>It’s tempting to judge progress by how smooth the drive feels. Smooth is nice. The better question is what they’re noticing and deciding without you.</p>
<p>Over time, a developing driver should get better at looking farther down the road, spotting trouble before it’s urgent, keeping a safe following distance, choosing speed for conditions and judging gaps. They should merge without freezing or making someone else brake. Change lanes the same careful way every time. See the pedestrian and the cyclist before you point them out. Let a missed exit go instead of making a sudden move to save it.</p>
<p>Every “watch that car,” “slow down” or “check your mirror” means you’re doing part of the driving. That’s exactly right while they’re learning. What matters is that you need to say it less over time.</p>
<p class="pull">The best sign of readiness isn’t how well they drive while you’re coaching. It’s how little coaching they need.</p>
</section>

<section class="q" id="how-to-teach">
<h2>How do I teach without becoming a driving instructor?</h2>
<p class="answer">Stop treating every drive as more time for the log.</p>
<p>You don’t have to recreate driver’s ed. Instructors have training you don’t, and they matter. Your job is different: the volume, the repetition and the variety that a handful of professional lessons can’t cover.</p>
<div class="bda">
<div><b>Before</b>Pick one thing. Following distance. Highway merges. A safe gap for a left turn. Narrow enough to actually focus on.</div>
<div><b>During</b>Coach when you need to, but don’t narrate every decision.</div>
<div><b>After</b>Two minutes. What felt hard? What surprised you? What did you notice too late?</div>
</div>
<p>When they make a bad call, figure out which kind it was before you correct it. Did they not see it? See it and misjudge it? Know what to do and react too slowly? Get distracted? Those are different problems, and they need different practice.</p>

