<section class="q" id="conflict">
<h2>What if practicing together turns into a fight?</h2>
<p class="answer">Some tension is normal. The goal isn’t a silent car. It’s a useful one.</p>
<p>Teaching your own kid to drive is a strange assignment. You’re trying to stay calm while someone you love learns a high-stakes skill in real time. They’re trying to learn while being watched by the person whose approval matters most. If you’ve ever stomped on the imaginary brake on the passenger side, you know the feeling.</p>
<ul>
<li>Give instructions early, not at the last second and at full volume.</li>
<li>Be specific about what needs to change.</li>
<li>Keep driving feedback separate from whatever else you’re arguing about this week.</li>
<li>If either of you is too frustrated to keep going safely, pull over and call it.</li>
</ul>
<p>And know the difference between a mistake and an attitude problem. New drivers will brake late, pick the wrong gap and drive you a little crazy. That’s why you’re there. The real concern is a kid who doesn’t notice their mistakes, won’t take coaching or acts like the risk doesn’t apply to them.</p>
</section>

<section class="q" id="is-drivers-ed-enough">
<h2>Is driver’s ed enough?</h2>
<p class="answer">No, and that’s not a knock on driver’s ed.</p>
<p>A good instructor builds the fundamentals, catches things a parent misses and can often get through to a teenager in a way a parent can’t. It’s one of the best tools you have. But instructor time is limited, and the experience it takes to become a capable driver builds over months and across a lot of different situations.</p>
<p>Driving instructors have shaped DRVN from the beginning for exactly this reason. They don’t see professional lessons and parent practice as competing. Each one needs the other.</p>
<p class="pull">Driver’s ed teaches. Practice with you turns it into experience.</p>

