<section class="q" id="road-test">
<h2>Doesn’t passing the road test prove they’re ready?</h2>
<p class="answer">It proves they met your state’s standard during one short test.</p>
<p>That matters. It just doesn’t cover much. No road test includes night driving, rain, an unfamiliar interstate, a tailgater, a blind rural curve or a chatty friend in the passenger seat. The test has to end. Real driving doesn’t.</p>
<p>The state is asking, “Has this person met the requirements for a license?” You still get to ask, “What are they ready to do with it?”</p>
</section>

<section class="q" id="ready-to-drive-alone">
<h2>How do I know when my teenager is ready to drive alone?</h2>
<p class="answer">Watch what they do, not how you feel.</p>
<p>Honestly, you’ll probably never feel completely comfortable watching them back out of the driveway alone the first time. Readiness doesn’t mean that feeling goes away. Instead, ask:</p>
<ul class="checks">
<li><span class="tick">?</span>Can they get through an ordinary drive without reminders about speed, following distance, signals, lane position, scanning or right of way?</li>
<li><span class="tick">?</span>Do they spot a problem forming before you point it out?</li>
<li><span class="tick">?</span>Can they handle something unfamiliar without getting overwhelmed?</li>
<li><span class="tick">?</span>When they make a mistake, can they keep from making a second one on top of it?</li>
<li><span class="tick">?</span>Do they know when conditions are beyond them?</li>
</ul>
<p>And the big one:</p>
<p class="pull">If you weren’t sitting there, would they have seen what you just saw?</p>
<p>If the answer is often no, keep practicing. Needing more time isn’t failing.</p>
</section>

<section class="q" id="after-the-license">
<h2>What changes after they get the license?</h2>
<p class="answer">The riskiest stretch starts when supervision drops off.</p>
<p>That’s why graduated licensing exists: it hands over independence in stages instead of all at once, and the CDC notes that these systems are proven to reduce crashes and deaths among young drivers.</p>
<p>You can use the same idea even where your state allows more. A new license doesn’t have to mean every road, every hour and every friend on day one. Start with familiar, easier solo trips. Add harder ones as experience builds. Keep doing the tough stuff together for a while.</p>
<p>The practice doesn’t have to end when the logbook does.</p>
</section>

<section class="q" id="teen-passengers">
<h2>What about teen passengers?</h2>
<p class="answer">Friends change everything in the car.</p>
<p>The CDC reports that an unsupervised teen driver’s crash risk goes up with teen or young-adult passengers, and keeps rising with each one added. Its guidance for parents recommends no teen or young-adult passengers for at least the first six months, when you can manage it.</p>
<p>Your state may allow more. You’re allowed to be stricter than the law.</p>
<p class="pull">A legal privilege doesn’t have to become a family privilege on the same day.</p>
</section>

<section class="q" id="night-driving">
<h2>What about night driving?</h2>
<p class="answer">Practice it on purpose before the license. Limit it on purpose after.</p>
<p>At night you see less, hazards show up later, everyone’s more tired, and it’s when a lot of social driving happens. Stack all of that on top of inexperience and you get the CDC number above.</p>
<p>The CDC suggests keeping newly licensed teens off the road by 9 or 10 p.m. for at least the first six months. That may be stricter than your state. It isn’t a punishment. It’s limiting time in the riskiest hours while experience catches up.</p>
</section>

<section class="q" id="phones-and-distraction">
<h2>What about phones and other distractions?</h2>
<p class="answer">A new driver doesn’t have much attention to spare.</p>
<p>Things you do on autopilot still take real thought for them, so a text, a playlist, navigation or a lively conversation makes a hard job harder.</p>
<p>“Don’t text and drive” is too small a rule. Try this one: <strong>the phone doesn’t get attention while the car is moving.</strong> Set navigation before you leave. Turn on driving focus. If something really needs attention, pull over.</p>
<p>And yes, they’ve been watching us drive for sixteen years. The rule we model will beat the rule we announce every time. (This one stings a little for all of us.)</p>
</section>

<section class="q" id="driving-agreement">
<h2>Should we have a parent-teen driving agreement?</h2>
<p class="answer">Yes. Most family driving arguments start with an assumption nobody said out loud.</p>
<p>Talk through passengers, night driving, phones, seat belts, alcohol and drugs, weather, where the car can go, who else can drive it, and what happens when a rule gets broken. Write it down before you need it.</p>
<div style="background: #0A6CA5; border-radius: 16px; padding: 24px 26px; margin: 6px 0 22px; color: #FFFFFF">
<div style="font-size: 12px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: .11em; text-transform: uppercase; color: #BFE7F8; margin-bottom: 8px">The rule above all the others</div>
<div style="font-size: 26px; font-weight: 800; line-height: 1.2; letter-spacing: -0.015em">Your kid can always call you for a safe ride home.</div>
<div style="font-size: 16.5px; line-height: 1.55; color: #E6F5FC; margin-top: 10px">If alcohol, drugs, exhaustion, an unsafe driver or a bad situation is involved, getting home safe is the only goal that night. The parenting conversation can wait until morning.</div>
</div>
</section>

<section class="q" id="state-minimum">
<h2>Should we follow the state minimum or go beyond it?</h2>
<p class="answer">Meet the minimum, because it’s the law. Go past it whenever your kid needs more.</p>
<p>Licensing rules vary a lot from state to state, even though learning to drive doesn’t get any easier at the border. IIHS best practices are more conservative than many states on permit age, practice hours, night limits and passengers.</p>
<p>The state has to write one rule for everybody. You only have to decide about one driver. That’s an advantage. Use it.</p>
</section>

<section class="q" id="doesnt-want-to-practice">
<h2>What if my teenager doesn’t want to practice?</h2>
<p class="answer">Then the license can wait.</p>
<p>Getting it at the earliest possible age isn’t a milestone anyone has to hit. If your teen dodges practice, avoids the hard stuff, brushes off coaching or isn’t showing good judgment, holding off is completely reasonable.</p>
<p>Wanting the license and being ready for it don’t always arrive together. Some kids want it badly before their skills are there. Others have the skills and are nervous to use them. The calendar doesn’t fix either one.</p>
<p>The goal isn’t a license at a certain age. <strong>It’s a capable driver.</strong></p>
</section>

<section class="q" id="anxious-teen">
<h2>What if my teenager is anxious?</h2>
<p class="answer">Anxiety doesn’t have to stop the process, but it should change the pace.</p>
<p>Build difficulty slowly. A nervous driver might need several drives in one setting before adding the next layer: a smaller shopping district before the busy one, the highway on a quiet Sunday morning before rush hour. Confidence should come from things going well, not just from being told it’ll be fine.</p>
<p>If the anxiety is bad enough that they can’t take in information or drive safely, a professional instructor offers a different setting and can help you figure out whether more support makes sense. There’s no prize for rushing.</p>
</section>

<section class="q" id="overconfident-teen">
<h2>What if my teenager is overconfident?</h2>
<p class="answer">Don’t argue about it. Change the scenery.</p>
<p>A few good months can convince a kid they’ve got this, and they stop thinking about everything they haven’t seen yet. Instead of debating whether they’re a “good driver,” go somewhere unfamiliar. Heavier traffic. Night. Rural roads. A tricky merge or a busy intersection.</p>
<p>Real skill holds up when conditions change. Familiarity usually doesn’t.</p>
</section>

<section class="q" id="readiness-check">
<h2>The goal isn’t the license</h2>
<p>To your teenager, a license means freedom. To you, after months of practice and years as the family taxi, it might mean relief. Both are fair.</p>
<p>But the license was never the point of the permit year. The point is getting to where your kid has seen enough, practiced enough and built enough judgment that you’re ready to let them make the calls without you.</p>
<p>That takes experience. Variety. Judgment. And a parent who stays in the passenger seat long enough for all three to show up.</p>
<p class="pull">The real question isn’t whether they can pass the test. It’s whether they can handle what comes after it, when nobody’s sitting next to them.</p>

<div style="border: 1px solid #DCE7EE; border-top: 5px solid #86B404; border-radius: 16px; padding: 24px 26px; margin: 10px 0 20px; background: #FFFFFF">
<div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: baseline; gap: 12px; flex-wrap: wrap; margin-bottom: 6px">
<h3 style="font-size: 24px; font-weight: 800">A quick readiness check</h3>
<button type="button" class="printbtn" onclick="window.print()">Print this checklist</button>
</div>
<p style="font-size: 16px; color: #45535E; margin: 0 0 10px">Before regular solo driving, your teen should be able to:</p>
<ul class="checks">
<li><span class="tick">✓</span>Control the car consistently</li><li><span class="tick">✓</span>Look well past the car directly ahead</li><li><span class="tick">✓</span>Spot hazards as they develop</li><li><span class="tick">✓</span>Adjust speed for conditions and keep a safe following distance</li><li><span class="tick">✓</span>Handle neighborhoods, commercial roads, highways and rural roads</li><li><span class="tick">✓</span>Drive at night and in reasonable bad weather</li><li><span class="tick">✓</span>Merge, change lanes and judge gaps without steady coaching from you</li><li><span class="tick">✓</span>Recover calmly from a mistake</li><li><span class="tick">✓</span>Leave the phone alone</li><li><span class="tick">✓</span>Recognize when a situation is beyond them, and ask for help instead of taking the risk</li>
</ul>
<p style="font-size: 16.5px; color: #45535E; margin: 0 0 8px">None of it has to be perfect. It just needs to belong more and more to them, and less to the person in the passenger seat. If you’re still supplying a lot of the judgment, that’s not failure. It means more practice.</p>
<p style="font-size: 17px; font-weight: 700; color: #212529; margin: 0">There’s very little downside to more supervised driving. There can be a big one to stopping too soon.</p>
</div>
<p>That’s the job you’ve been handed. DRVN exists to make it a little easier.</p>
</section>

<section style="margin-top: 36px; padding-top: 18px; border-top: 1px solid #E7EEF3">
<div class="eyebrow">Where this guidance comes from</div>
<p style="font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; color: #6B7A85; margin: 10px 0 8px">DRVN’s safety guidance leans on NHTSA, the CDC, the Insurance Institute for Highway Safety and the Center for Injury Research and Prevention at Children’s Hospital of Philadelphia, along with peer-reviewed research and official state sources. We’re clear about which is which: what the law requires, what the research shows and what we’d recommend as parents who’ve been through it. Always check your state’s requirements against its official source. <a href="/about/#standards">Our editorial standards</a></p>
<p style="font-size: 14px; color: #6B7A85; margin: 0"><b style="color: #2E3B45">Last reviewed:</b> October 2026</p>
</section>

</article>
